{"causes":["The compilation of the Man'yōshū was influenced by the rise of Buddhism in Japan during the 8th century.","The collection was compiled during a time of great cultural and artistic achievement in Japan, with the influence of Chinese and Korean poetry.","The compilation of the Man'yōshū was also influenced by the reign of Emperor Shōmu, who oversaw the compilation of many important works of Japanese literature."],"effects":["The Man'yōshū has had a lasting impact on Japanese literature and art, influencing many poets and writers for centuries.","The collection has been an important source of inspiration for Western poets and writers, who have drawn on its imagery and themes in their own work.","The Man'yōshū has also played a significant role in the development of Japanese culture, influencing the way that people think about and express themselves."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Nara period","sourceName":"Nara period","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"Man'yōshū","targetName":"Man'yōshū","context":"The Man'yōshū was compiled during the Nara period, which was a time of great cultural and artistic achievement in Japan."},{"sourceSlug":"Ōtomo no Yakamochi","sourceName":"Ōtomo no Yakamochi","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"Man'yōshū","targetName":"Man'yōshū","context":"The famous poet and scholar Ōtomo no Yakamochi assisted in the compilation of the Man'yōshū."},{"sourceSlug":"Chinese poetry","sourceName":"Chinese poetry","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Man'yōshū","targetName":"Man'yōshū","context":"The Man'yōshū was heavily influenced by Chinese and Korean poetry of the time."},{"sourceSlug":"Emperor Shōmu","sourceName":"Emperor Shōmu","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"Man'yōshū","targetName":"Man'yōshū","context":"The compilation of the Man'yōshū was also influenced by the reign of Emperor Shōmu, who oversaw the compilation of many important works of Japanese literature."},{"sourceSlug":"Buddhism in Japan","sourceName":"Buddhism in Japan","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Man'yōshū","targetName":"Man'yōshū","context":"The compilation of the Man'yōshū was influenced by the rise of Buddhism in Japan during the 8th century."},{"sourceSlug":"Western literature","sourceName":"Western literature","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Man'yōshū","targetName":"Man'yōshū","context":"The Man'yōshū has been an important influence on Western literature, with many poets and writers drawing on its imagery and themes in their own work."}],"places":[{"name":"Japan","role":"Birthplace"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Man'yōshū","type":"Reference article","slug":"manyōshū"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q834321","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27y%C5%8Dsh%C5%AB"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Man'yōshū contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Man'yōshū (Japanese: 万葉集, 'Collection of Ten Thousand Leaves') is the oldest surviving anthology of Japanese poetry, compiled c. 759 CE (during the Nara period, 710–794 CE) and attributed to the compiler Ōtomo no Yakamochi (c. 718–785 CE), though the anthology was assembled over a longer period and includes poems by multiple editors. The Man'yōshū contains approximately 4,516 poems in three main forms — the chōka (long poem), tanka (short poem, 5-7-5-7-7 syllables), and sedōka — composed by poets from the Emperor and court nobles to frontier guards and commoners, spanning approximately 130 years of Japanese literary history (c. 629–759 CE). It is written primarily in Man'yōgana, a system of using Chinese characters to represent Japanese phonetic syllables, predating the development of kana.

The Man'yōshū is the foundational text of the Japanese literary tradition — its approximately 4,500 poems established the tanka (and its descendant the waka) as the primary form of Japanese poetry, a form that would dominate Japanese literary culture from the Nara period through the modern era. The anthology's aesthetic sensibility — its celebration of the natural world (the seasons, mountains, rivers, birds), its directness and freshness of feeling, its expression of love, separation, and longing — established the aesthetic values (makoto, sincerity; aware, the pathos of things) that would characterise Japanese poetry for centuries.

The Man'yōshū's cultural significance in modern Japan is extraordinary — it was used by the Meiji Restoration's imperial ideologues as evidence of the Emperor's cultural centrality and the purity of Japanese culture before Chinese influence; and in May 2019 the name of the new imperial era Reiwa (令和, 'beautiful harmony') was taken from the preface to poem 5 in Book 5 of the Man'yōshū ('Under the beautiful harmony of the sky, with a gentle spring breeze, the plum blossoms open like powder before a mirror') — the first imperial era name drawn from a Japanese rather than a Chinese source.","subjects":["Japanese Literature","Ancient Era","Nara Period","Tanka Poetry","Waka","Japanese Poetry Anthology","Ōtomo no Yakamochi","Man'yōgana"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Man'yōshū has had a lasting impact on Japanese literature and art, influencing many poets and writers for centuries.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
